# Personal config for source-intake-agent.
# Copy this to `.env` (which is gitignored) and edit for your machine.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env
#   ./install.sh         # or ./install.sh --link
#
# Variables already exported in your shell environment when you invoke
# install.sh take precedence over what's set here.

# Free-text description of what this library covers. Used to give claude
# domain context for category choices, section framing, and tone. Be
# specific — "AI/LLM research papers and policy reports" beats "research".
# A few examples:
#   "AI/LLM research papers, technical reports, and legal-AI scholarship."
#   "Recipes from cookbooks, blogs, and magazines, oriented toward home cooks."
#   "Tech industry news articles and analyst reports from 2024 onward."
#   "Climate policy briefings and academic papers on energy transition."
#   "Personal reading notes from non-fiction books across the humanities."
DOMAIN="A general-purpose personal research library."

# Where intake summaries are filed.
LIBRARY=~/source-library

# The folder to drop new sources into.
INBOX=~/source-library-inbox

# Reverse-DNS prefix used in launchd plist labels.
LABEL_PREFIX=com.user

# Optional. Path to a reference-book repo to triage new sources against.
# When set, each successful intake runs a second claude pass (stage 2)
# that decides where in the reference book the new source is relevant —
# does it change an existing claim, add material, or have no effect —
# and writes a report to $REFBOOK/triage/<category>--<slug>.md. Reports
# are proposals only: stage 2 never edits the reference book's docs.
# Leave unset to disable stage 2 entirely.
# REFBOOK=~/Developer/ai-refbook
#
# Stage-2 runtime tunables (set in ~/.config/claude-source-intake/env,
# alongside MODEL and CLAUDE_TIMEOUT — not here):
#   TRIAGE_MODEL=...     model for the triage pass; defaults to MODEL
#   TRIAGE_TIMEOUT=600   wall-clock seconds per triage run

# Override the path to the claude CLI binary. Default: $(command -v claude).
# CLAUDE_BIN=/opt/homebrew/bin/claude

# Optional preferred display order for category folders in INDEX.md and
# the dashboard's Library page (comma-separated). Categories not listed
# are appended alphabetically.
# CATEGORY_ORDER=foo,bar,baz

# Optional. Set to your email to opt into OpenAlex's "polite pool" (higher
# rate limit, friendlier errors) for the weekly preprint-publication check.
# Strongly recommended if your library has more than a handful of arXiv or
# SSRN sources. Email is sent only to api.openalex.org.
# OPENALEX_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Optional. Comma-separated list of additional browser origins permitted to
# make state-changing requests to the dashboard (CSRF allowlist). 127.0.0.1
# and localhost on the dashboard port are always allowed. Use this when you
# front the dashboard with a reverse proxy or a custom hostname. Each entry
# must be a full origin: scheme://host[:port], no trailing slash.
# DASHBOARD_EXTRA_ORIGINS=https://library.example.localhost,https://library.example.com

# Optional. Localhost port the dashboard binds to.
# DASHBOARD_PORT=7341

# Optional. Preprint cache entries older than this many days are re-checked
# by the weekly cron and the dashboard's "Check now" button.
# PREPRINT_REFRESH_DAYS=7

# Optional. User-Agent sent when fetching candidate sources. The default is a
# browser UA because some hosts (CourtListener, americanbar.org, other
# WAF-fronted sites) 403 the bare "curl/x.y" UA but serve a normal 200 to a
# browser. Override only if a source needs a specific UA.
# FETCH_USER_AGENT="Mozilla/5.0 ..."

# Optional. Smallest fetched body (bytes) accepted as a real document. The
# default of 1 rejects only empty responses — e.g. EUR-Lex answering an async
# request with HTTP 202 + 0 bytes, which curl reports as success. Raise to e.g.
# 512 to also discard tiny anti-bot "challenge" pages.
# FETCH_MIN_BYTES=1
